  "query_preview": "..\nTonight through Tuesday: Currently, a band of moisture is \nstretching along the Continental Divide from Canada to Mexico. \nFor the Inland Northwest, it is generating low cloud shield over\nEastern Washington and North Idaho. It is not expected to \ngenerate much in the way of precip. Amounts are expected to be a\nhundredth of an inch or less. A drier, stable air mass will \nfill in behind the band on Tuesday Temperatures will be cooler \nthan Monday. Highs will be in the 40s. Overnight lows will be in\nthe upper 20s and 30s. \n\nWednesday through Saturday: Start of Wednesday will continue \nthe cool, dry pattern from Tuesday. A low will begin press into \nthe West coast late Wednesday. The upper level jet continues to \nsteer the bulk of moisture and energy from the Low south of the \nPacific Northwest. Ensembles are in decent agreement with the \ntrack. A weak deformation band swings into the region as the Low\npushes further Inland. Precip will spread across the region \nthrough Saturday. The 25-75% probability precip amounts range \nfrom 0.05-0.3 of an inch for the Thursday through Saturday \nperiod. Snow levels will dip to 3000 ft allowing for a chance of\nsnow. Little to no accumulation is expected. As this low exits \non Friday, another one will be right on its heels for Saturday. \nHighs continue to be in the 40s. Overnight lows will be in the \nupper 20s and 30s.\n\nSunday and Monday: Ensembles are bringing a system into the \nPacific Northwest Coast. Southern Oregon is the likely landing \nspot. It will keep the higher precip amounts south of the Inland\nNorthwest. It leads to another round mountain snow showers with\npossible accumulations up to 3 inches. Temperatures will \ncontinue to cool. Highs will be in the 30s to low 40s. Lows will\nbe in the 20s to low 30s. /JDC",
